STANDING RULE, widened 9 Nov 2026 by the owner's instruction:

  HOW A LOG IS READ MUST BE FORMAT-AGNOSTIC. THE OLD RULE READ ONE PRODUCER'S WORDING.

  The rule as it stood read exactly two patterns:
      grep -c -E '^HIT[[:space:]]+index'      <- detection
      grep -E '^exit='                        <- completion
  Both are solver2_jac's output format and nothing else's. Measured 9 Nov 2026:
    brain_image_corpus.py:471-475   prints "  *** n HIT ***" and "commit=<sha256>"
    old_electrum.py, t12_family_check.py   have formats of their own
    brain_image_corpus.py            prints no exit= line at all when run directly;
                                     exit= comes from the run_queue_*.sh wrappers
  '^HIT[[:space:]]+index' returns 0 for every one of those producers INCLUDING ON A REAL
  HIT, because the string it searches for is never printed. image_corpus_run.log scored 0
  under the old rule and would have scored 0 with a hit sitting in it.

  WHY IT MATTERS. A hit-detection grep keyed to one producer's wording is a check that
  prints a number nobody made it earn. That is the same class as REPORT 1.7, 1.8 and 1.9,
  and it is the worst version of it, because the number it prints is the one number the
  HIT protocol depends on.

  THE RULE NOW. Two greps, neither tied to a producer:
      grep -c -i -w -E 'hit|hits|commit=|phrase[[:space:]]*:'  <log>    # detection
      grep -E '^exit=|acceptance|NO MATCH|VOID'                <log>    # completion
  Nothing else in a log is read. show_hit is never called. A NONZERO DETECTION COUNT
  STOPS EVERYTHING and is reported before any other work, whatever the completion grep
  says. A zero detection count is only a negative once the completion grep shows the run
  finished: for solver2_jac "exit=2", for brain_image_corpus.py "acceptance ... EXACTLY
  1.0" with no "VOID" (its own guard at lines 463-466 voids any run whose acceptance is
  below 1.0, because a brainwallet has no checksum and so acceptance below 1.0 means work
  was silently dropped).

  ON THE WORD ANCHORS (-w). The owner's instruction gave the detection grep unanchored,
  as -i -E 'HIT|commit=|phrase[[:space:]]*:'. Run over all 100 logs in both trees, the
  unanchored form fires on ordinary text, because HIT is a substring of other words:
    queuenov_t12full_mp65.log   2 matches   SHIT, FUCKTHISSHIT (passphrase candidates)
    image_corpus_run.log        3 matches   whitepaper x2, verify_hit
  The anchored form scores 0 on both, and still returns 2 on each of st_selftest_find.log,
  st_selftest_uncomp.log and st_selftest_hybrid.log -- the three planted POSITIVES, which
  are the only logs in either tree that contain a real hit. Those three are the anchored
  rule's positive control: it is not merely quiet, it is quiet where it should be and loud
  where it should be. A rule that cries wolf on the word "whitepaper" gets ignored, which
  is the same failure in the opposite direction. The anchors were added for that measured
  reason; the format-agnostic requirement is the owner's.

